\author{Compiled by Claude Sonnet 5 (claude-sonnet-5) for Andr\'as Kornai}
\date{}
\begin{document}
\maketitle

\section{Introduction}
Toposa (ISO 639-3 \texttt{toq}; Glottocode topo1242) is an Eastern Nilotic language of South Sudan. Speaker estimates in the search results differ widely: Wikipedia gives roughly 320,000 (2017) and Omniglot about 100,000. The search found little published material.

\section{Primary Educational Resources}
\begin{itemize}
\item A 2021 UNICEF request for proposals covered literacy primers for primary 2 and 3 in five South Sudanese languages. I could not confirm that Toposa is one of them. \url{https://www.ungm.org/Public/Notice/133550}
\end{itemize}

\section{Linguistic and Reference Materials}
\begin{itemize}
\item Wikipedia article on the Toposa language. \url{https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Toposa_language}
\item Omniglot page on the Toposa alphabet and pronunciation. \url{https://omniglot.com/writing/toposa.htm}
\item Glottolog entry for Spagnolo, \emph{Toposa grammar and dictionary} (undated). \url{https://glottolog.org/resource/reference/id/151013}
\end{itemize}

\section{Religious and Community Resources}
\begin{itemize}
\item Wycliffe Toposa project profile (2016--2018), covering Bible and film goals. \url{https://www.wycliffe.org/Main%20Uploads/Projects/PDF/Toposa%20Profile%20WEB.pdf}
\item Wikipedia article on the Toposa people. \url{https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Toposa_people}
\end{itemize}

\section{Digital and Audio Resources}
No audio or software was found in the search results.

\section{Curriculum Development Context}
South Sudan's General Education Act 2012 makes the mother tongue the language of instruction in P1--P3, according to a search summary; I did not check the Act itself. No Toposa textbook was found. The Spagnolo grammar and dictionary is a possible starting point, but I could not confirm that it is available.

\end{document}
